00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:32.160
one of the most enjoyable spy movies ever,

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:36.040
Esquire ranked him as the
best Bond baddie in history.

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:38.880
Connery also squared off
against the original incarnation

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:41.330
of Blofeld, played by Donald Pleasance,

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:44.240
but to me, this round
belongs to Roger Moore's

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:47.270
unique array of villains that
boasts diversity of thought,

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:50.120
tactics, intelligence and quirks.

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:53.000
Unless Pierce Brosnan wants to swoop in-

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.880
- What? No small talk?

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:56.490
No chitchat?

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:57.850
- He does make a case

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:00.100
with "Goldeneye" as both Shawn Beam

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:02.860
and Fomka Jansen make terrific villains,

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:05.630
but after that, as much
as I love Jonathan Price,

00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:07.950
the bad guys just can't compete.

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:10.730
This round belongs to Roger Moore's 007,

00:12:10.730 --> 00:12:13.993
and now our tie at the top is a three way.

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:18.110
- Original. What will they think of next?

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:21.770
- And now it's time for
our wildcard round gadgets.

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:22.603
Yep, that's right.

00:12:22.603 --> 00:12:24.690
It's the round that throws
a monkey into the wrench,

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:27.840
a twist into the fate and
a shake into the martini.

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:31.420
Which Bond is bringing the
best gadgets to his mission?

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:35.620
Every 007 film features tons
of fun technological toys,

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:38.630
so I'll stick with each
player's very best of the best.

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:40.900
These are my favorite gaggle of gadgets

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:42.400
from the one and only cue,

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:44.670
shout out to the late great Desmond Lewin,

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:47.510
who portrayed the role of
Bond's genius sidekick the most,

00:12:47.510 --> 00:12:49.290
and to John Cleese and Hugh dancy

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:51.470
who've made the role their own sense.

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:54.240
All right, let's explode some pens.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:55.703
- Oh grow up 007.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:58.940
- This might be the only
round where Sean Connery

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:00.870
is at somewhat of a disadvantage

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:04.250
because his golden Bond
years were back in the 1960s.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:06.610
However, he still gets
to show off some gear

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:08.460
that would make Boba Fett blush.

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:10.960
We'll get this jet pack in "Thunderball".

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:12.980
I'm also a fan of the cigarette gun,

00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:15.090
but both those things are dangerous, kids.

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:17.250
From "You Only Live Twice" and a suitcase

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:20.420
that probably inspired that
mariachi from Desperado

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:21.800
in "From Russia with love".

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:25.070
Roger Moore's avant garde 70s
approach to both his fashion

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:28.149
and tech up the ante as we
were introduced to everything

00:13:28.149 --> 00:13:32.230
from a wrist gun to a
bug detecting shaver.

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:34.410
You know, for those hard to reach places

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:36.060
where you might have bugs.

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:38.470
But if I'm highlighting
one technological marvel

00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:40.830
from Moore's run, it's a watch.

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:43.700
They can also be a saw.

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:45.960
Oh, did we mention it's a Rolex?

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:48.580
Yeah, go back and
revisit "Living Let Die".

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:50.220
Now I like the Guns and Roses version

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:52.040
over Paul McCartney's hot take,

00:13:52.040 --> 00:13:54.890
but there is no way to
improve upon a wristwatch,

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:57.240
they can also help you build a deck.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:00.270
Good luck replicating that one, apple.

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:02.360
Pierce Brosnan scores some major points

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:04.360
for his GoldenEye exploding pen.

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:07.620
But in his later films, the
gadgets tried a little too hard

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:09.750
to pay homage to the goofier 70s.

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:12.423
- So, this is where they
keep the old relics?

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:16.370
- We had a disappearing
car, the X ray glasses.

00:14:16.370 --> 00:14:18.320
It was fun, do not get me wrong,

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:20.450
but it also made clear
that this Bond iteration

00:14:20.450 --> 00:14:23.350
was only going to get
sillier and more ridiculous

00:14:23.350 --> 00:14:26.030
even as Pierce did his
best to add legitimacy

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:27.820
to the zaniness surrounding it.

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:31.820
So exit Remington Steele
and enter layer cake.

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:33.853
- We were expecting an exploding pen.

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:38.110
We didn't really go in for that anymore.

00:14:38.110 --> 00:14:40.690
- Daniel Craig's 007
robot is much on his fist

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:41.910
and the occasional headbutt

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:43.870
as much as any previous super spy,

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:46.270
but he still had some clutch gadgetry

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:48.400
that helped literally keep him alive.

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:51.010
The defibrillator
narrowly avoided his death

00:14:51.010 --> 00:14:53.430
in his very first 007 outing.

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:55.740
We also saw sweet explosive key chain,

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:58.240
which is classic bond,
and then in "Specter",

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:01.900
we saw some scientific
tomfoolery with the smart blood.

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:03.140
Also let's not overlook

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:06.130
the return of the Aston
Martin in "Skyfall".

00:15:06.130 --> 00:15:08.190
People lost their minds in the theater

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:09.430
when it was revealed.

00:15:09.430 --> 00:15:12.240
Even me who questionably
prefers Pierce Brosnan's

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:15.600
Zippy BMW Z3 from "Goldeneye".

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:16.940
After that movie came out,

00:15:16.940 --> 00:15:20.020
it became the midlife crisis card elise,

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:21.600
move over Mazda Miata,

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:23.780
there's a new tiny sheriff in town.

00:15:23.780 --> 00:15:26.930
The BMW Z3, perfect for saving the world

00:15:26.930 --> 00:15:30.220
from evil geniuses and run by fritters.

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:33.840
Every bun has a cool car, great
gadgets and fists of fury,

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:35.680
including George Lazenby,

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:37.750
who will unfortunately
taken an L in this round

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:40.630
simply due to the lack of a tech resume.

00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:42.290
- This never happened to the other fella.

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:45.190
But what his one movie lacks
in Geek Squad know how,

00:15:45.190 --> 00:15:48.680
he makes up for with
actual fighting ability.

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:50.040
His martial arts on the beach

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:52.860
and "On Her Majesty's Secret
Service" are impressive.

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:55.230
So maybe he didn't care
about the more modern toys.

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:58.050
Folks who do their yoga
claim their bodies a temple,

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:00.330
Lazenby's Bond body was a gadget,

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:02.660
and he wielded it with vengeance.

00:16:02.660 --> 00:16:05.370
That leaves Timothy Dalton
who would seem to be limited

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:08.200
by his mere two films in the world of 007,

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:11.210
but those flicks came out in the 1980s

00:16:11.210 --> 00:16:13.050
and thus presented the best mixture

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:15.050
of classic bond gadget necessity

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:17.350
with futuristic tech ridiculousness.

00:16:17.350 --> 00:16:20.490
His Aston Martin come standard
with a rocket launcher,

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:23.370
and he also has a ride that
could ski down mountains.

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:26.260
Plus every great vehicle
needs an exploding key fob

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:28.980
and his license to kill
signature gun is pure,

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:31.890
unfiltered 007 mythology.

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:34.330
Dalton ended up turning
down a contract extension

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:36.680
after a prolonged legal
battle over the rights

00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:39.360
to James Bond movies, but his two entries

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:42.580
added so much to the Bond gadget portfolio

00:16:42.580 --> 00:16:44.220
that we wanted to see more.

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:46.890
And in the end, that's
enough to get him a surprise

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:48.470
winning the wild card round,

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:53.470
meaning it's a one to
one to one to zero tie.

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:55.080
- Wherever she was must have scared

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:56.770
the living daylights out of her.

00:16:56.770 --> 00:16:59.360
- Hell of an effort by all six gentlemen.

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:02.037
An honorable mention goes to the 1967

00:17:02.037 --> 00:17:04.770
"Casino Royale" star, David Nibin.

00:17:04.770 --> 00:17:06.970
George Lazenby came into a top decision

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:08.650
as he followed a legend in the making

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:11.640
in Sean Connery, but he
adjudicated himself well

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:14.480
and kept the franchise afloat
for Roger Moore to come in

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:16.490
and give us the next great era.

00:17:16.490 --> 00:17:18.710
And props to Pierce
whose "Goldmine" may be

00:17:18.710 --> 00:17:20.010
one of the most important films

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:22.440
in the entire legacy of Bond.

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:24.840
Without his name, power
and the vintage Bond feel

00:17:24.840 --> 00:17:27.030
of that movie, we may not be referring

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:30.670
to 007 as a present day
force at the cinemas.

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:33.660
But Lazenby and Brosnan
couldn't muster quite enough

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:35.120
to win a round today,

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:36.940
so now my decision for the winner

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:40.920
works between Connery,
Moore, Dalton and Craig.

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:42.300
And speaking of rounds,

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:45.040
let's make grownup
hydration the tiebreaker.

00:17:45.040 --> 00:17:47.620
Our own expert researcher Mark Hoffmeyer,

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:49.410
who I would say is the cue of this show,

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:51.610
except he looks much
better in a suit than I do,

00:17:51.610 --> 00:17:54.090
referred me to the
economist who broke down

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:58.220
all the on camera drinks
James Bond has consumed.

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:02.500
The most exercise liver of
the bunch goes to Daniel Craig

00:18:02.500 --> 00:18:05.100
with 22 total cocktails.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:06.900
Connery is second with 16,

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:09.720
and Moore follows closely with 14.

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:13.470
And for me, I want my Bonds
brand to be well lubricated,

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:16.070
but also sober enough
to pull off the mission,

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:21.070
which is why Sean Connery is
still my ultimate James Bond.

00:18:21.670 --> 00:18:23.190
We only lost him recently

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:25.420
but he got a head start on everyone else

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:26.970
and never looked back.

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:31.970
- So, do you lose as
gracefully as you win?

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:35.670
- I wouldn't know, I've never lost.

00:18:35.670 --> 00:18:38.140
- It's Connery's mix of masculine swab,

00:18:38.140 --> 00:18:40.880
rough handsomeness and
pitch perfect one liners

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:42.290
that sealed the deal for me.

00:18:42.290 --> 00:18:45.250
Some of the content in his
era hasn't aged all that well,

00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:48.450
but Mr. Connery in the suit is timeless.

00:18:48.450 --> 00:18:50.210
Unlike this vid, which could be outdated

00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:53.700
as soon as I see Daniel
Craig's last stand as 007.

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:55.810
Maybe he returns to "Skyfall" forum,

00:18:55.810 --> 00:18:58.090
in which case I'd have
to return to the studio

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:00.390
and rent this from Men's Warehouse again

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:02.450
to reconsider my take.

00:19:02.450 --> 00:19:03.970
So while that keeps me up at night,

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:05.640
we leave it to you, dear viewer,

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:06.940
to weigh in on the subject

00:19:06.940 --> 00:19:09.750
and let us know your
favorite Bond of all time.

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:12.280
You can hit me up anytime
at Mark Ellis live,

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:14.140
and while you're at it, show MI6

00:19:14.140 --> 00:19:17.740
that you care by subscribing
to Rotten Tomatoes on YouTube

00:19:17.740 --> 00:19:20.300
and checking out rottentomatoes.com.

00:19:20.300 --> 00:19:23.110
Thanks for watching, and I
do believe it's time for me

00:19:23.110 --> 00:19:26.103
to also have a drink that
is shaken, not stirred.

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:28.410
In which case I'll then have to clean up

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:30.824
all the course light that
explodes out of the can.

00:19:30.824 --> 00:19:33.407
(upbeat music)

